---
layout: layouts/tool.njk
toolSlug: slug-generator
title: "URL Slug Generator: SEO-Friendly Slugs"
description: "Turn titles into clean, lowercase URL slugs: accents removed, spaces and punctuation become hyphens, with stop-word removal, max length and bulk mode."
permalink: /tools/slug-generator/
h1: "URL Slug Generator"
extraCss: "/tools/slug-generator/style.css"
quickAnswer: "A URL slug is the readable part of a web address that identifies a page, such as percentage-change-calculator. To make one, lowercase the title, remove accents, replace spaces and punctuation with hyphens and trim the ends: 'Héllo, Wörld! 2026' becomes hello-world-2026. Keep slugs short and descriptive, and do not change them once a page is published."
formula:
  title: "How a Slug Is Built"
  expression: "Title → NFD normalise and remove accents → lowercase → non-letters and non-digits become the separator → collapse repeats → trim the ends"
  explanation: "Unicode NFD normalisation splits letters such as é into e plus an accent mark, and the marks are removed. Letters that do not split (ß, æ, ø, ł) are written out as ss, ae, o and l, and & becomes 'and'. Everything that is not a letter or a digit becomes a single separator, and separators at the start or end are removed. Optional steps drop common English stop words and cut the slug at a word boundary so it stays within a maximum length. Letters from non-Latin scripts are kept, because browsers display them, but you may prefer to translate the title first."
  variables:
    - name: "Separator"
      description: "Hyphens (-) are the usual choice for URLs; underscores (_) suit file names and some systems."
    - name: "Stop words"
      description: "Short words such as a, the, and, of, to that rarely help a URL. At least one word is always kept."
    - name: "Max length"
      description: "Cuts the slug before the word that would go over the limit, so words are never chopped in half."
workedExamples:
  - title: "Accents and punctuation"
    inputs:
      - "Title: Héllo, Wörld! 2026"
    steps:
      - "Remove accents: Hello, World! 2026"
      - "Lowercase and replace punctuation and spaces: hello-world-2026"
    result: "hello-world-2026"
  - title: "Extra spaces and underscores"
    inputs:
      - "Title: '  Multiple   Spaces  '"
      - "Separator: hyphen, then underscore for 'Hello World'"
    steps:
      - "Runs of spaces become a single separator and the ends are trimmed"
    result: "multiple-spaces, and hello_world with the underscore separator"
  - title: "Maximum length at a word boundary"
    inputs:
      - "Title: percentage change calculator"
      - "Max length: 10"
    steps:
      - "'percentage' is 10 characters; adding '-change' would make 17"
    result: "percentage"
commonMistakes:
  - title: "Changing the slug of a live page"
    description: "A new slug is a new URL. Links, bookmarks and search results that point to the old address break unless you set up a permanent (301) redirect. Choose the slug carefully before publishing."
  - title: "Stuffing keywords into the slug"
    description: "best-cheap-free-calculator-online-calculator-tool reads as spam. A short, accurate slug that matches the page title is easier to read and share."
  - title: "Using dates that will go out of date"
    description: "A slug like best-laptops-2024 looks stale the following year, and changing it later means a redirect. Leave dates out unless the page is about a specific year."
  - title: "Mixing upper and lower case"
    description: "Paths on many servers are case-sensitive, so /About-Us and /about-us can be different pages. Lowercase slugs avoid duplicate URLs."
  - title: "Using underscores in web addresses"
    description: "Google's guidance recommends hyphens to separate words in URLs; words joined by underscores can be read as a single word."
faqs:
  - q: "What is a URL slug?"
    a: "It is the part of a web address that names a specific page, usually after the last slash. In toolsbuilt.com/tools/slug-generator/ the slug is slug-generator."
  - q: "Should I use hyphens or underscores in URLs?"
    a: "Use hyphens. Google recommends hyphens to separate words in URLs, and they are the common convention. Underscores are fine for file names."
  - q: "Should I remove stop words like 'the' and 'and' from slugs?"
    a: "Often, yes, when it makes the slug shorter without losing meaning, such as write-best-guide instead of how-to-write-the-best-guide. Keep them if the slug reads badly without them."
  - q: "How long should a URL slug be?"
    a: "There is no fixed limit, but three to five meaningful words is a good target. Use the max length option to keep slugs within a limit without cutting words."
  - q: "How are accents and special characters handled?"
    a: "Accents are removed with Unicode normalisation, so é becomes e and ü becomes u; ß becomes ss, and & becomes 'and'. Other symbols turn into the separator."
  - q: "Can I make slugs for many titles at once?"
    a: "Yes. Use Bulk mode and paste one title per line. Turn on unique numbering to add -2, -3 and so on to repeated slugs."
  - q: "Does changing a slug affect SEO?"
    a: "Changing the URL of an existing page means search engines must find the new address. Use a 301 redirect from the old slug so links and rankings carry over."
sources:
  - name: "Google Search Central: URL structure best practices"
    url: "https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/url-structure"
  - name: "RFC 3986: Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) Generic Syntax"
    url: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3986"
  - name: "Unicode Standard Annex #15: Unicode Normalization Forms"
    url: "https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr15/"
  - name: "Google Search Central: Redirects and Google Search"
    url: "https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/301-redirects"
lastReviewed: "2026-10-31"
howto:
